details:
In MidiPlayer, if the final note is being played and it lasts more than 1 frame, the MIDI processing/event emitting loop is broken out of and never fires the OnBlockEnd (i.e. MidiNoteOff), delaying it until the entire audio file ends (i.e. time seeks to the start, which seems to be how you're detecting the end of playback) - and not at all if the audioSource is set not to loop.

Please ensure MIDI processing in  playback does not terminate until *all* blocks have finished playing, otherwise MIDI playback will be inconsistent.

Easy reproduction case:

- Get a MIDI file + audio file

- Set up udon with the following code in Start() (to avoid needing to craft a MIDI file by hand):

```

player.MidiData.tracks = new MidiData.MidiTrack[1];

player.MidiData.tracks[0] = new MidiData.MidiTrack();

player.MidiData.tracks[0].blocks = new MidiData.MidiBlock[1];

player.MidiData.tracks[0].blocks[0] = new MidiData.MidiBlock();

var block = new MidiData.MidiBlock();

block.startTimeMs = 1000;

block.endTimeMs = 1500;

block.channel = 1;

block.note = 82;

block.velocity = 127;

player.MidiData.tracks[0].blocks[0] = block;

player.Play();

```

(Alternatively, create a MIDI file with a single note near the start)

You will notice that the MidiNoteOff never fires when endTimeMs is hit, because flag is false in `MidiEnumerator` at this point.
